initiatives/init-run-measurement.md:74:| 2 | 2026-09-08 | update | Feasibility and decomposition attached by the lead-solutions-architect role at initiative-check's attach-architecture step. The full offer, in the role-offer type's parts. *role* — lead-solutions-architect. *verdict* — feasible as framed, for a run the router moves; bounded elsewhere. Reasons: the outcome asks for mechanical data gathering only — no model, rows and nothing else, exactly the no-gos. Two of the four fields are already within reach through work this shop has already done: a run's context is recorded on its anchor at the run's end (feat-process-runner v9, the scenario "the run's context is recorded on the anchor at its end"), and the delivered router already reads the harness's own usage report at finer grain than that scenario requires — per resumed segment and per launched role, both on haiku and on fable (feat-process-runner's Document History v9: "the lane run 40 turns, cache-read 1.69M on haiku... the launched roles 1.15M on fable"). Minutes and tool uses need no model either: a runtime step reads timestamps and tool-invocation counts off the same anchor and transcript the router already reads, deterministically. Output tokens are the one field the framing itself already qualifies ("where the harness gives them"): the cost analysis that opened this whole initiative had to estimate them from characters, "the transcripts carry only a streaming stub" (req-2026-09-07-run-efficiency, §Evidence) — so a row that leaves this field blank is not a shortfall, it is the scoped answer. Not feasible without qualification: a session that no approved process definition moved through the router — most sessions run today, including the one that opened this check — carries no anchor with per-step values to read, so nothing mechanical exists yet to key a row on; "every session" is reachable only as the sibling init-process-runner's rollout reaches every session's work, and that initiative's own measure is not yet met (4.6M router context for one small change, feat-process-runner v9). *decisions* — D1, decision: "The one row per agent run is written to a new artifact kept beside the session record — never as an amendment to the session-record schema, which this shop does not own (`pkg:shopsystem-knowledge/session-record`, fetched through that package's own contract tool). The row is written by a runtime step, never an agent step: it reads the run's anchor, where the run passed through the router, and the harness's own usage report; a field the harness does not expose for a given run (most often output tokens) is left blank, never estimated and never computed by a model. The step's home is session-handoff-process's `collect` step, the process that already produces the session record this artifact sits beside; whether reconcile-and-close's own close and a review conversation's anchor need the same step is a separate question, not decided here." Under this role's guardrail right, the same right adr-2026-09-07-coordinator-role stands on; reversible — the artifact and the step are removed without changing what either process's typedef promises elsewhere; record: none. D2, decision: "The initiative's own words scope the target to the session-record anchor — 'beside the session record' — not to a review record or a work item; a row for those two anchors, if wanted, is a separate request against reconcile-and-close and the review conversation's own anchor, not folded into this bet's appetite." Under this role's decomposition right (which activity carries which capability); record: none. *risks* — R1: output tokens and tool-use counts are confirmed available only by hand, from a full transcript read (the cost analysis this initiative answers did exactly that); if the harness's own usage report does not carry a per-tool-use count the way it carries per-turn cache-read tokens, that field is blank on every row until a transcript-parsing tool is built — enabler work, not this attach step's to commit. R2: most sessions today are not router-moved (this very check is one), so the "every session" target is coupled to a sibling initiative's rollout the authority has already bet on but not yet met; a bet here that assumes full coverage on day one overstates what ships. R3: two close processes exist (session-handoff, reconcile-and-close) and review conversations have no anchor process defined yet (this branch's own primer names the reconcile-side amendments as open); a step added to session-handoff alone leaves work and review conversations uncounted, which D2 scopes out rather than hides. R4: the new artifact must reference the session record's own frontmatter (id, created, authors) rather than restate it, or the two records drift apart under `single-source-of-truth`; the maker's discipline at build, flagged here so the build is checked against it. *unknowns* — U1: whether "minutes" means wall-clock, agent-minutes, or both; the cost analysis this initiative answers tracked both as distinct columns. Default: wall-clock only, read off the anchor's own timestamps, since agent-minutes needs summing turns the anchor does not yet keep separately. U2: the new artifact's exact name and location. Default: one file per session record, named to pair with it, in `sessions/`, the maker's choice at build. U3: whether "one row per agent run" counts a runtime step (no agent, no minutes of its own). Default: no — a row per agent or human step and per top-level router/lead-pm turn only. U4: whether review and work conversations are in the target's "every session." Default: no, per D2 — the framing's own words name the session record alone. *evidence* — this initiative (v1); the parent init-run-efficiency (v1) and its measure; req-2026-09-07-run-efficiency (v3), its cost table and the four places it names the cost sits, read in full; sess-2026-09-07-b, the session that framed this initiative and the sibling bet; basis/processes/session-handoff.md (v3) and basis/processes/reconcile-and-close.md (v4), both naming the router and producing, respectively, the session record and a work item's close; basis/glossary.md's `anchor`, `session record`, and `conversation type` entries — three conversation types, three anchors, one of which (review) names no process yet; feat-process-runner (v9) in full, its context-at-end scenario and its delivery history rows v7–v9, the harness's usage-report evidence at segment and role grain; adr-2026-09-07-coordinator-role (v3, checked), the guardrail this offer's right matches; basis/contexts/ — absent, confirming no Bounded Context exists on this branch. The feature repository swept in full — seven other features (feat-request-routing, feat-role-decisions, feat-roles-availability, feat-skills-availability, feat-tool-skills, feat-tool-skills-rest, feat-typedef-rendering) read for conflict: none names a session record, a cost row, or a close step; no conflict. Principles screen: `knowable-shape` — no first-class entity added, the new artifact is a lead-shop-held record, not a Bounded Context or a shop; `contracts-between-contexts` — not touched, no Bounded Context in scope; `actor-neutral-discipline` — the row records the same fields for a run whoever or whatever model tier moved it; `local-comprehension` — the artifact sits at the coordinating level beside the record it measures, readable without opening a Bounded Context; `bidirectional-conformance` — D1 and D2 are recorded here before any step is added to session-handoff-process, so the amendment is a design change first, not drift; `intent-provenance` — traces to req-2026-09-07-run-efficiency and sess-2026-09-07-b, both already recorded. Maker's evaluation against the role-offer type and the initiative typedef's §4–5: the four list parts each carry entries, no `none`; the verdict carries reasons; §4 rendered short with the designer's part left "not yet"; §5 attached with the flow "none"; `record: none` twice, each with its reason stated inline since this is the first pass; the offer names no Bounded Context's internals — basis/contexts/ does not exist on this branch. |
